{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TiDsjzwesThcZU3U2RYCoB","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient measurement temperature dependence cm2/s density 800 K\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Pressure induced Structure Change and Anomalies in Thermodynamic Quantities and Transport Properties in Liquid Lithium Hydride\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.15532\"},{\"title\":\"Lithium Intercalation in the Anisotropic van der Waals Magnetic Semiconductor CrSBr\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15663\"},{\"title\":\"Structural and transport properties of LiTFSI/G3 electrolyte with machine-learned molecular dynamics\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.20243\"},{\"title\":\"m.math-net.ru\",\"url\":\"https://m.math-net.ru/eng/ftt11863\"},{\"title\":\"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium\",\"url\":\"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/\"},{\"title\":\"s00231 011 0874 9\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9\"},{\"title\":\"Self-diffusion in liquid lithium from coherent quasielastic neutron scattering\",\"url\":\"https://katalog.lib.cas.cz/KNAV/EdsRecord/edsair,edsair.doi...........2430b859cd2c8ede718b24c249f43fd0\"},{\"title\":\"peeref.com\",\"url\":\"https://peeref.com/works/14514882\"},{\"title\":\"m.math-net.ru\",\"url\":\"https://m.math-net.ru/eng/tvt693\"},{\"title\":\"Fizika Tverdogo Tela\",\"url\":\"https://www.mathnet.ru/eng/ftt11863\"}]\n\nThe search didn't turn up a measured self-diffusion value for liquid lithium at 800 K in cm²/s, so I can't give that number from these results.\n\n- **Experimental work:** Neutron-scattering studies cover this range. One group measured liquid lithium's self-diffusion over roughly 500–830 K using coherent quasi-elastic neutron scattering (Blagoveshchenskii, Novikov, and Savostin). The same group's 2014 follow-up also addressed lithium and lead, per Fizika Tverdogo Tela and Physics of the Solid State (2014). The underlying data tables weren't in the results, so I couldn't pull the 800 K value.\n- **Older experiments:** Murday and Cotts (1968, *J Chem Phys*) is the classic measurement, and a 2012 simulation paper reported that its results agree well with Murday's experiment results in atmosphere.\n- **Simulation:** An embedded-atom-potential molecular dynamics study reported that self-diffusion coefficients under isobaric heating increase with temperature by the power law with exponent of 1.7182. That is a power law, not an Arrhenius form, so it differs from the 2012 paper's approach.\n- **Trend:** The 2012 simulation found that the liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient increases with temperature increasing and decreases with pressure increasing.\n\nTo get the 800 K number, I'd suggest pulling the full text of the Blagoveshchenskii et al. paper (Physics of the Solid State 2014, or the Physica B 2012 coherent-scattering paper), or Murday and Cotts (1968), and reading the tabulated values or fitted Arrhenius parameters directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f3d38ce5-fd50-4c31-85ee-32a266c2561f","uuid":"feca5b29-d2ad-4bb9-b9f0-d643eb68262c","timestamp":"2026-11-01T02:58:43.356Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient measurement temperature dependence cm2/s density 800 K","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01ApocHozvWe2tDFjJAN5g2Z","content":[{"title":"Pressure induced Structure Change and Anomalies in Thermodynamic Quantities and Transport Properties in Liquid Lithium Hydride","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.15532"},{"title":"Lithium Intercalation in the Anisotropic van der Waals Magnetic Semiconductor CrSBr","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15663"},{"title":"Structural and transport properties of LiTFSI/G3 electrolyte with machine-learned molecular dynamics","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.20243"},{"title":"m.math-net.ru","url":"https://m.math-net.ru/eng/ftt11863"},{"title":"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium","url":"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/"},{"title":"s00231 011 0874 9","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9"},{"title":"Self-diffusion in liquid lithium from coherent quasielastic neutron scattering","url":"https://katalog.lib.cas.cz/KNAV/EdsRecord/edsair,edsair.doi...........2430b859cd2c8ede718b24c249f43fd0"},{"title":"peeref.com","url":"https://peeref.com/works/14514882"},{"title":"m.math-net.ru","url":"https://m.math-net.ru/eng/tvt693"},{"title":"Fizika Tverdogo Tela","url":"https://www.mathnet.ru/eng/ftt11863"}]},"The search didn't turn up a measured self-diffusion value for liquid lithium at 800 K in cm²/s, so I can't give that number from these results.\n\n- **Experimental work:** Neutron-scattering studies cover this range. One group measured liquid lithium's self-diffusion over roughly 500–830 K using coherent quasi-elastic neutron scattering (Blagoveshchenskii, Novikov, and Savostin). The same group's 2014 follow-up also addressed lithium and lead, per Fizika Tverdogo Tela and Physics of the Solid State (2014). The underlying data tables weren't in the results, so I couldn't pull the 800 K value.\n- **Older experiments:** Murday and Cotts (1968, *J Chem Phys*) is the classic measurement, and a 2012 simulation paper reported that its results agree well with Murday's experiment results in atmosphere.\n- **Simulation:** An embedded-atom-potential molecular dynamics study reported that self-diffusion coefficients under isobaric heating increase with temperature by the power law with exponent of 1.7182. That is a power law, not an Arrhenius form, so it differs from the 2012 paper's approach.\n- **Trend:** The 2012 simulation found that the liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient increases with temperature increasing and decreases with pressure increasing.\n\nTo get the 800 K number, I'd suggest pulling the full text of the Blagoveshchenskii et al. paper (Physics of the Solid State 2014, or the Physica B 2012 coherent-scattering paper), or Murday and Cotts (1968), and reading the tabulated values or fitted Arrhenius parameters directly."],"durationSeconds":5.961894058999999,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TiDsjzwesThcZU3U2RYCoB","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
